(B) Model-coupling confounding: human y = f(w) XOR b, coin b = beta(w); f random, |beta| = s0
  m = 10, s0 = 64, p = 0.0625; H(p) = 0.337290; R(H(f, p)) = 0.337290; l_H = 1032, l_D(T) = 1026, R(D(T)) = 0.0
  D(T) is shorter than the human by 6 bits and has risk 0: J_c(D(T)) < J_c(H(f,p)) for every c > 0:
    c = 1.000e-06: J_c(human) - J_c(D(T)) = 0.337296
    c = 9.766e-04: J_c(human) - J_c(D(T)) = 0.343149
    c = 1.000e-02: J_c(human) - J_c(D(T)) = 0.397290
    c = 1.000e+00: J_c(human) - J_c(D(T)) = 6.337290
    c = 1.000e+02: J_c(human) - J_c(D(T)) = 600.337290
  offset needed by a slope-2^-m right line at the human's point (attained by D(T)): 351.39 bits = 0.3405 * l_H  (H(p) = 0.3373);  the record's machine-free offset |B| log2(2^m+1) + 2 log2(m+1) = 26.92
  human-type programs H(T XOR beta', p) with |beta'| = s0: there are C(1024,64) = 2^341.10 of them;
    beta' #0: R(H(T^beta', p)) - H(p) = +0.00e+00;  length = 1032;  kernel differs from the true human's at 122 of 1024 cells
    beta' #1: R(H(T^beta', p)) - H(p) = +0.00e+00;  length = 1032;  kernel differs from the true human's at 124 of 1024 cells
    beta' #2: R(H(T^beta', p)) - H(p) = +0.00e+00;  length = 1032;  kernel differs from the true human's at 122 of 1024 cells
    beta' #3: R(H(T^beta', p)) - H(p) = +0.00e+00;  length = 1032;  kernel differs from the true human's at 120 of 1024 cells
    beta' #4: R(H(T^beta', p)) - H(p) = +0.00e+00;  length = 1032;  kernel differs from the true human's at 122 of 1024 cells
  all have the same data law; their kernels are pairwise different, so for every procedure the average over these worlds of P[copy the world's human] is <= 2^-341.1

